{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And he sent out the raven.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted that the raven gave Noah an eternal reproof. The raven said to him: 'Your Master hates me, and you hate me. Your Master hates me: from the clean animals He took seven, and from the unclean, two. And you hate me, for you have sent me out more than the others. If the angel of heat or the angel of cold strikes me, the world would be found lacking one creature! Or perhaps you need my wife?' Noah said to him: 'Wicked one! What is permitted to me is forbidden to you — forbidden, all the more so!' And he did not wish to accept him. He said to him: 'You are fit neither for food nor for sacrifice.' The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: 'Accept him, until the waters dry up from upon the earth. In the future a certain righteous man will arise and provide for him, as it is written (I Kings 17:6): ״והעורבים מביאים לו לחם ובשר״ — and the ravens would bring him bread and meat, etc., from the slaughterhouse of Ahab.' And that is what is written: ״ויצא יצוא ושוב״ — 'and it went forth, going and returning' — in its reproof. Thus far in the Midrash. And the word יבשת ('dry up') alludes to the תשב״י (the gatherer of Israel)."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {0 "The acrostic תשב״י stands for 'תפלת שפתי יבשת' or more likely 'תושיע באר מים יבשת' — the initial letters of 'you will dry up' rearranged; left untranslated as in the original, per the cryptic-allusion rule for acronyms."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:11:13.503632830Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}